\f1\fs22 The command post occupied a broad octagonal room with a cluster of workstations facing each other in the middle while cabinets stood like pawns and rooks against the walls under the dull sheen of inoperative panoramic screens. Eloise sat at what Hayley took to be the main console, judging by the sole fact that the chair before it looked more important than all the others. \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 As the screens lit, Hayley leaned forward. Eloise moved her chair aside to let her reach the controls.\
\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A Shakti emblem appeared, then a stylised graphic design of Eloise\'92s face.\
\'93Is that necessary?\'94 Hayley asked.\
\'93Not my idea. Never underestimate the sycophancy of the powerless.\'94\
Hayley chewed as if trying to swallow boot leather. \'93You look younger.\'94\
\'93It\'92s an old system. Also\'85\'94\
Hayley finished the sentence. \'93Never underestimate the sycophancy\'85\'94\
Eloise\'92s face disappeared. Views of the compound populated the walls: the entrance, the hover pad, the office park, the Traverse station. Hayley pulled up a chair as Eloise popped up selected camera angles on the three screens before her.\
\'93Can we get sound?\'94\
Eloise tapped an icon on a screen. An Elvis filled the room halfway through a chorus. \'93There\'92s ear pieces in the drawer.\'94\
\'93May I?\'94\
Eloise acquiesced.\
Hayley leaned over, took the mouse and toured the menus on the screen. She found a map of Sublime Point that she could drill down on and find camera positions. It seemed like no matter how far she zoomed, the display remained afflicted by a multi-coloured pox of dots. \
\'93There are short-cuts,\'94 Eloise explained.\
\'93I\'92m sure I\'92ll find them,\'94 Hayley said while she kept working. She dragged boxes around the Traverse station until she could click on individual cameras. Tiles of high-definition surveillance stacked up on the screen.\
The station was still guarded, if not by the detail who helped them into a Traverse car, then a shift of the same number.\
Hayley brought up the carpark, and found views of a lit-up stage and a substantial crowd. She found angles on the compound gates where a protest seemed to be working people up to attempting something silly. Hayley saw a woman carrying a sleeping child.\
\'93Who ordered the gates shut?\'94\
\'93Automated assessment, most likely.\'94 Eloise checked and nodded.\
\'93Can we lower the gates from here?\'94\
\'93Of course. But are you sure you know what we might be letting loose on the village if we did? Are you sure what the UDF out there will do?\'94\
\'93We can\'92t keep people in forever.\'94\
\'93And we won\'92t. But let\'92s not make a decision we can\'92t reverse just yet.\'94\
Hayley swallowed. She called up cameras in the auditorium. Emergency lighting picked out empty seats, discarded programmes and production workers moving equipment. The shark tank was still there.\
Chip appeared in the doorway with the plate of cheese, biscuits and dried fruit. And a bottle of wine.\
Eloise glanced at him. \'93Didn\'92t I lock you up?\'94\
\'93Ancient history,\'94 Chip said through a mouthful that was far from pleasant to observe. He moved the keyboard of the neighbouring station and set the provisions down.\
\'93I want that weapon,\'94 Eloise said.\
Chip unscrewed the wine. \'93I know. But Fridge Force is going to stop you.\'94\
\'93What can they do?\'94\
Chip shrugged. \'93More than you\'92d give them credit for.\'94 \
\'93Where did you find refreshments, anyway?\'94\
\'93I have skills.\'94\
\'93I don\'92t doubt it.\'94 Eloise took a cracker. \'93Did you bring glasses?\'94\
Eloise was aghast when Chip drank straight from the bottle.\
\'93Nope.\'94
